#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084021} :activity/id #uuid "a68b3f7f-449b-5dcc-876b-2a9dda0b7af9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060 :topic/id #uuid "d6922e01-c712-5ba1-b304-de1c9c2ec2dd" :topic/math-academy-id 742} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084514 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without using graphs*, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=1 \\\\ 5x-y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we must combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation. One way to combine the equations is to use the **elimination method.** \n\nNotice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-1.$ Since these numbers add to zero, we can *add* the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{y} & =1 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =11 \\\\ (x+5x) & =(1+11) \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (2)+y & =1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(2, -1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48f030ba-6728-565a-be53-cfb1e88abc86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8702 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22b878ba-aa9a-5218-889c-006c8dcacca7" :step/math-academy-id 143346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082932 :question/id #uuid "06136eef-284e-548f-a57b-f400a05f2cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13993"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e0dbf68-2247-5a47-aac8-c16acc5e710c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496321}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} :step/id #uuid "5234accd-01b6-501e-866d-11b931397534" :step/math-academy-id 143347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082933 :question/id #uuid "17cb905e-e692-59b8-964d-c7b50482c11e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e562941a-078d-555f-aab6-c59d4c79dfba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559861}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/id #uuid "adba60f0-2f20-5985-8599-c86e9c30f9db" :step/math-academy-id 143351 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084515 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that we want to solve the following system of linear equations using elimination:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/85/85996afab1889f8032a295f4db6bbbcbc8670a0bc12f0b64f53a3b809f111628.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This eliminates the variable $x,$ making it possible to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "170be181-ae77-50d7-a5f1-0a4bdcfa318e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8704 :tutorial/title "Eliminating Variables From Systems of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "1c45a64c-2352-5510-9c77-6d975e32ee8a" :step/math-academy-id 143354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082934 :question/id #uuid "98645504-986a-5847-8042-32ced1cf4d14" :question/math-academy-id "e-13996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc55e7f0-598a-5244-84ef-6dff74f0c420" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559823}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Result of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "f6f3680b-161b-5ba4-b393-bb9b5b36236b" :step/math-academy-id 143355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082935 :question/id #uuid "0e96914f-2f28-5fef-8a36-9cf2869b1b43" :question/math-academy-id "e-14000"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e61a36-c5bf-598a-a07d-833611ffaee6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559874}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b6308dc5-5a40-5abe-9f8e-91524b3b2359" :step/math-academy-id 143359}] :activity/title "Introduction to the Elimination Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083433}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559944} :activity/id #uuid "397850c6-acc7-5579-8f02-292fa0842dbf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060 :topic/id #uuid "d6922e01-c712-5ba1-b304-de1c9c2ec2dd" :topic/math-academy-id 742} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e5070a-60a7-51db-a7a0-ecbd3b937e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559028} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfdc83e-36c8-5bab-b817-c6b95dba314d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b788af03-66b4-5f39-9af4-1a154adb18e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559475} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4124ec5-3e6f-5d7a-9254-21f518b1c2eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d0d1a49-c8c0-5ac4-a633-472aeac1d8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214839" :question/problem "Using the elimination method, eliminate the variable $p$ and find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4p+q=-24 \\\\ -4p+7q=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $4,$ and the coefficient of $p$ in the second equation is $-4.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $p,$ and solve the resulting equation for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4p}+q & =-24 \\\\ -\\cancel{4p}+7q & =24 \\\\ (q+7q) & =(-24+24) \\\\ 8q & =0 \\\\ q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $q=0.$ However, we still need to find $p.$ To do this, we substitute $q=0$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p+q & =-24 \\\\ 4p+(0) & =-24 \\\\ 4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-6,\\,q=0,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,-\\,6,0\\,).$"} :step/id #uuid "48662e2a-320d-5b65-83c0-53722452ce12"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79e9ed81-8adc-5412-8b2e-0655de8c1d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559321} {:answer/id #uuid "801bd6ee-55fe-555a-a5a1-7e40518e7c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559334} {:answer/id #uuid "a20dfd08-1474-5e67-86b1-3cce1d26279a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559425} {:answer/id #uuid "d924bb24-e255-5e91-950b-1728e67a3a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559548} {:answer/id #uuid "f76338d9-5322-5de7-be10-ca730f0460ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559321} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cd66a4-4798-5107-a762-4b88b1221841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e48aeb5c-60a2-50ba-8aad-37ecd4e919eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-170020" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5t+2v=13 \\\\ 2t+v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the second equation by $-2$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}-4t-2v=-10.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}⋯\\,\\,?\\,\\,⋯\\,\\,=3.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(t,v)$ of the system." :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-2$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5t+2v=13 \\\\ (\\,2t+v=5\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5t+2v=13 \\\\ -4t-2v=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $v,$ and solve the resulting equation for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t+\\cancel{2v} & =13 \\\\ -4t-\\cancel{2v} & =-10 \\\\ (5t-4t) & =(13-10) \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $t=3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $t=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+v & =5 \\\\ 2(3)+v & =5 \\\\ 6+v & =5 \\\\ v & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $t=3,\\,v=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "7e438f98-2e65-5923-a09a-5e8be95eb6f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d16b812f-c6b5-520d-969e-18bfaa8a836f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559529} :answer-field/id #uuid "61df3c66-7c8a-518e-88c5-f9c95caa3752" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559699} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6432dec-a821-5a8f-91c0-bc8eb104b4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559622} :answer-field/id #uuid "92216407-d4a0-5405-80ce-75bdb22713b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d011b84-399d-5ccd-8fd8-17c37c9d5011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559087} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfc01578-2ddc-5e45-a0ad-a9e131bb6c30" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a91b43fb-d787-5c95-8cec-baa3db0b3a5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-208822" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y=9 \\\\ 2x-y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $y$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the second equation by $5$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}10x-5y=5.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$=14.$\n\n$\\,$\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $5$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y=9 \\\\ (\\,2x-y=1\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y=9 \\\\ 10x-5y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+\\cancel{5y} & =9 \\\\ 10x-\\cancel{5y} & =5 \\\\ (-3x+10x) & =(9+5) \\\\ 7x & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =1 \\\\ 2(2)-y & =1 \\\\ 4-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-3 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,2,3\\,).$"} :step/id #uuid "810b9fa4-db24-548c-ad22-6936f48bccc2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dfaf16c-d8c2-5fee-b8aa-4c4defa51d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6922ba3e-81d0-5c06-979c-0240b56ed179" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7611515b-eb94-5d0e-a6cc-97bdcad28000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559313} :answer-field/id #uuid "702844cc-350c-5557-8e47-27b87222f0d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd2697ea-50eb-59a5-a69d-76e0004cb7aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-214764" :question/problem "Using the elimination method, eliminate the variable $x$ and find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=45 \\\\ -5x-7y=-70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $5,$ and the coefficient of $x$ in the second equation is $-5.$ \n\nSince these numbers add to zero, we can add the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $x,$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{5x}+2y & =45 \\\\ -\\cancel{5x}-7y & =-70 \\\\ (2y-7y) & =(45-70) \\\\ -5y & =-25 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=5.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =45 \\\\ 5x+2(5) & =45 \\\\ 5x+10 & =45 \\\\ 5x & =35 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=7,\\,y=5,$ which can be expressed as $(\\,7,5\\,).$"} :step/id #uuid "b651d884-2534-5e34-b6eb-6c5d837c21b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559922}}] :activity/title "Introduction to the Elimination Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559002}]}
